### Platform-linked image (`--format platform-linked`, seed module 78)

Context word 408 = 3 (ADR 0371 step 5), x86_64-linux only, dispatched by
seed/73 (and by seed/78-full-entry in the combined compiler F); only the GPU
provider is built this way. The runtime text is
`xs-rt-core` followed by `xs-rt-pstart`. The image is ELF64 ET_EXEC for the
host's dynamic linker, with 7 program headers in this order: PT_PHDR (64, 392
bytes), PT_INTERP (`/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2` at 456), LOAD RX [0, 4096 +
code) as in the static image, LOAD RW at D (page after the code) with filesz
4096 + 512 and memsz 4096 + 65536, PT_DYNAMIC at D, PT_GNU_RELRO [D, D +
4096), PT_GNU_STACK RW. After the program headers: a hash table with one
empty bucket (nchain = the symbol count), the symbols (GLOBAL FUNC, undefined),
the strings, and the relocations. At D: the dynamic section (NEEDED per
library, HASH, STRTAB, SYMTAB, STRSZ, SYMENT, RELA, RELASZ, RELAENT, FLAGS =
DF_BIND_NOW, FLAGS_1 = DF_1_NOW, NULL). The GOT is at D + 2048 (8 bytes per
import), and D + 4096 is the context (cell 195), so the static fixups and the
context image are unchanged. Imports are a fixed table: 0
`__libc_start_main` (libc.so.6) always; 1 `vkGetInstanceProcAddr`
(libvulkan.so.1) only when the program calls `kxrt-p-vkgipa` (cell 204).
R_X86_64_GLOB_DAT relocations only. Entry `_start`: `xor ebp,ebp; mov
r9,rdx; pop rsi; mov rdx,rsp; and rsp,-16; push rax; push rsp; xor r8d,r8d;
xor ecx,ecx; lea rdi,[kxrt-start]; lea rax,[context]; call [rax-2048]; ud2`,
that is `__libc_start_main(kxrt-start, argc, argv, 0, 0, rtld_fini,
stack_end)`. `kxrt-start(argc, argv, envp)` maps the arenas, fills the v11
context, reads `KEXE_ALLOW`, `KEXE_FUEL`, `KEXE_STRUCTURED_REPORT`,
`KEXE_CAP_RESOURCES_35` and `KEXE_ARENA_USE` as a static image does, calls
`main`, flushes, and returns `result & 0xff` (or 0 after the structured
line) to libc, which exits. There are no signal handlers, limits or seccomp:
a runtime refusal is an unhandled SIGILL. Intrinsic 9, `kxrt-ccall fp argv`
(cell 200, mode 3 only), loads rdi rsi rdx rcx r8 r9 from argv[0..5] and
copies argv[6..13] to [rsp..rsp+64) (the frame's outgoing area). It then sets
eax = 0, calls fp and reloads r9 = context. The program may call the
runtime's `kxrt-p-` functions: `ccall`, `vkgipa` (GOT[1]), `got-slot` (the
address of GOT[0..1]), `alloc` (anonymous RW mmap), `load8`/`store8`/
`load64`/`store64`, and raw `read`/`write`. seed/78 checks these calls by
name; any other runtime function is E7101.

For the GPU provider (ADR 0371 step 6) the API also has wrappers of fixed
syscalls, each returning -errno on failure (there is no generic syscall):
`clock-ns` (CLOCK_MONOTONIC in ns), `ignore-sigpipe` (SIGPIPE to SIG_IGN),
`open-ro path` (openat AT_FDCWD, O_RDONLY|O_CLOEXEC), `fsize fd` (st_size from
fstat), `pread fd a n off`, `close fd`, `unmap a n` (munmap, for memory from
`alloc`), `uname a` (struct utsname at a), SHA-256 streamed over a 64-byte
state st in the program's memory: `sha256-start st`, `sha256-blocks st a n`
(whole 64-byte blocks; another n is -EINVAL) and `sha256-finish st a n total
out` (the last n bytes, the total length; the 32-byte digest at out; returns
32), and `sha256 a n out` (the three at once),
`landlock-abi`, `landlock-ruleset fs-access` (handled_access_fs only),
`landlock-path ruleset path access` (a path-beneath rule; the path is opened
O_PATH|O_CLOEXEC and closed), `landlock-restrict ruleset` (no_new_privs, then
landlock_restrict_self; the ruleset fd is closed), and `seal`. Seal installs
ADR 0371 Decision 5's filter with SECCOMP_FILTER_FLAG_TSYNC, after
no_new_privs: another arch or an x32 number kills the process; execve,
execveat, fork, vfork, socket other than AF_UNIX, and clone without
CLONE_THREAD fail with EPERM; clone3 fails with ENOSYS (libc then uses clone);
everything else is allowed. It returns 0, the id of a thread that could not be
synchronized, or -errno. Landlock restricts the calling
thread and the threads it creates afterwards only: threads started earlier
(a driver's, during device creation) keep their file access, and only the
seal (TSYNC) covers them. Paths
are NUL-ended bytes in memory. The wrappers' scratch is a 16 KiB page that
`kxrt-start` maps at G+16704 in this format only.

## aarch64-linux-static (seed modules 77, 80)

`compile SOURCE --target aarch64-linux --output NEW --format static|runner|launcher`
(and `--format packed --payload KSEED`, below) (Linux selfhost closure;
dispatched by 73) emits the same image shape for
EM_AARCH64. Module 80 is a plain stack-machine emitter for the runtime-profile
SIR, independent of the adopted 41-a64gen: every local and temp lives in the
frame (sp-relative, the x86 frame sizes), x0..x4 carry arguments 0..4 and
arguments 5.. go to [sp, 8*(i-5)], x7 holds the context at every fuel site
and guest call, helpers are `ldr x16,[x0,#slot]; blr x16` with x0 = context.
Generated code uses only x0..x17, x29, x30 and sp, so runtime functions are
AAPCS64-callable from a foreign payload. Fixups are MM-FIX kinds 11..17
(imm26 FN/LABEL, imm19 LABEL, absolute FN/data/literal as movz+3 movk,
code-relative literal). Entry: `mov x0, sp; bl kxrt-start; brk #0`. Traps are
`brk #0` (SIGTRAP, exit 120) for guest traps; the runtime's own refusals (the trap
intrinsic) are `udf #0` (SIGILL), as the loader's helpers raised SIGILL. The runtime (77) is generated from the same
template as 76: syscall numbers, openat for open, setitimer for alarm, O_
flags, AUDIT_ARCH_AARCH64 and the aarch64 allow-lists differ. The runner
format takes `PAYLOAD OFFSET 0 aarch64 ALLOW [-- ARGS]` and enters the payload
as the loader did: fn(0..0, x7 = context). `KEXE_STACK_BYTES` (both arches)
sets the stack budget; the adopted AArch64 compiler needs 8 MiB.

### Packed images (`--format packed --payload KSEED`)

SOURCE is the runner source. The code of KSEED's exported `main` (a `.kseed`
container, as `extract-native` reads it; `main` must take no arguments, else
E7105) follows the image's code at a page boundary inside the RX segment.
Context word 408 = 2, and words 416/424/432 hold the payload's address,
length and entry offset. At start the runtime sets words 384/392 (the code
range) to the payload and enters it as the runner enters a mapped payload,
with the static image's command line, environment and result (`KEXE_ALLOW`
from the environment, exit status = result). Packing the adopted core's G3
gives a standalone optimized compiler: it rebuilds the core to the same
container (`selfhost-bootstrap.py` step 3b). Modes 0 and 1 emit 0 in words
416..432, so their images are unchanged.

## wasm32-browser-static/v1 (JUN-353)

`compile SOURCE --target wasm32-browser --output NEW` (seed/81, runtime text
seed/82) uses the same admission and static-build cells (197..199, 200..255
for the slot functions; kxrt-slot-400 is the trap handler, called directly).
Mode 3 never occurs on this route, so it reuses these cells:

| Cell | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 194 | pool length |
| 195 | defined function count |
| 201 | current basic block |
| 202 | block count of the current function |
| 203 | table size |
| 205 | fixup record of the current body |

FF-CODE of a FN holds, from the low bits up:

- bits 0..31: the wasm function index;
- bits 32..47: the table slot + 1;
- bits 48 and up: an inline kind (1 = `memory.grow`, 2 = `memory.size`).

The MM-FIX records serve as lists:

| Kind | Fields | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| 30 | start, end, f | a function body |
| 31 | slot, f | a thunk |
| 32 | start, end | the thunk's body |
| 40 | start, end, id | a section payload |

Linear memory:

| Address | Contents |
|---|---|
| 4096 | the context (fuel at +8, the allow bitmap at +16, code_base at +384 = the pool's address, code_length at +392) |
| 5120 | G |
| 65536 | the literal pool (a data segment) |
| after the pool, 64 KiB aligned | the runtime's arena (memory.grow) |
